WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.620
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:11.040
Today we are going to talk about the unfortunate&nbsp;
fortunate situation of computer animation in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:17.760
video games. Now I hear you asking, Doctor,&nbsp;
what do you mean by unfortunate fortunate?

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:23.880
Well, let’s start with the unfortunate part.&nbsp;
If we have created a beautiful virtual world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:29.220
and we have populated it with a bunch&nbsp;
of characters, we now wish to see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:36.600
two interact. And therein lies the problem. With&nbsp;
previous techniques, either the quality of motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:43.980
is lacking, or generality. In other words, if the&nbsp;
motions are of high quality, they don’t transfer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:50.340
to new scenes, or if they do transfer to new&nbsp;
scenes, they are typically not high quality.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:56.820
So that is the unfortunate situation. Now&nbsp;
what about the fortunate part? What is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:03.420
fortunate? Well, I feel very fortunate&nbsp;
today. Get this. I seem to be the fourth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:09.360
person to have read this paper. And I am&nbsp;
very excited to say that I think I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:15.360
found a little gem to share with you that might&nbsp;
just solve the problem we have at hand. Look.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:23.640
This one builds on top of the Adversarial Motion&nbsp;
Priors (AMP) paper from SIGGRAPH 2021. This paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:29.220
could place a virtual character in a virtual&nbsp;
environment, and it looked at a dataset of real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:36.060
clips of movements like running, jumping and&nbsp;
rolling, and even punching, and it was able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:42.420
learn to use those motions in these video game&nbsp;
worlds to its advantage to finish a level. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:49.080
what about this new technique? Does it do what&nbsp;
we really want to it to do? Yes, yes it does!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:56.100
Fantastic. It builds on top of this AMP technique&nbsp;
and generalizes it so that these characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:02.340
will not just run around in the scene, but it&nbsp;
teaches them to interact with the scene as well.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:07.620
So what does this mean? Why is&nbsp;
this new? Well, because one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:14.220
this is finally able to create motions that&nbsp;
are both high quality as the one you can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:22.380
here as it is encouraged to solve these tasks&nbsp;
with style. And two, this one also generalizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:28.080
to new objects and environments at&nbsp;
the same time. An absolute miracle.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.200
This was trained with a lot&nbsp;
of different object types,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.360
orientations and locations. And when I&nbsp;
say that they can do this with style,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:44.400
I mean that they can do this with style. Look&nbsp;
at this jolly chap. He is fabulous. Love it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:50.760
And these objects are newly designed objects that&nbsp;
the AI hasn’t seen yet. And it can still use them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:58.620
just fine. High-quality motion is being created&nbsp;
in a way that finally generalizes. So good!

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:04.800
And this AI has undergone strict testing,&nbsp;
and the scientists found that we can put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:11.340
any kind of box anywhere, this little AI&nbsp;
will be able to bring it back. I also love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:17.160
this scene which shows a variety of different&nbsp;
motions for walking towards this object and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.780
sitting on it. If only they have made&nbsp;
a musical chairs version of it where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:27.540
one of these poor little characters will be&nbsp;
eliminated each round. May the best AI win!

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:34.200
And, are you thinking what I am thinking? Do&nbsp;
you know what is coming up now? Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:40.680
testing against external perturbations. Oh&nbsp;
yes, the favorite pastime of the computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:47.520
graphics researcher. Look. These folks are&nbsp;
not only robust, but super patient as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:53.640
Look at these nasty tricks the scientists&nbsp;
tried to pull on them. Not a single one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.640 --> 00:04:02.220
worked. So good! Loving it! Look…whoop!&nbsp;
Whoop! Alright, you get to sit down now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:10.980
Whoop! Sorry, I had to. Okay, you can sit&nbsp;
now. Good job! A+ for patience, little AI!

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:16.440
Now, not even this technique is perfect,&nbsp;
the success rates are not a hundred percent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:23.160
but it is typically above 90% and I&nbsp;
am fairly sure this can be improved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:30.300
or at the very least be filtered in a way&nbsp;
that it approaches nearly a 100% for real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:36.180
applications. If we start messing with them,&nbsp;
like throwing boxes at them or tripping them up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:43.740
this success rate falls by about 3 to 10%.&nbsp;
That is very respectable, especially given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:49.860
by how badly we have treated them. Sorry about&nbsp;
that. But we got to proceed with the testing.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:56.700
So, in a new paper that only 4 people had&nbsp;
seen yet, we finally get this age-old problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:02.340
in computer animation solved. I can’t&nbsp;
believe it. What a time to be alive!

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:00.840
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

